.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Every quantum yield needs the number of photons absorbed. Hatchard and Parker (1956) introduced potassium ferrioxalate as a sensitive chemical photon counter: light reduces Fe(III) to Fe(II) with a known quantum yield (about 1.21 at 365 nm), and the Fe(II) is measured by the red phenanthroline complex at 510 nm (~chemistrykit.photochem.ferrioxalate_fe2_moles). Dividing by the quantum yield and the irradiation time gives the lamp&#x27;s photon flux (~chemistrykit.photochem.ferrioxalate_photon_flux). Here synthetic readings from a series of irradiation times are converted back into the flux of a lamp of known output.">

